A1. A fellow with very taking ways. ~ ROBBER
A6. To attack with argument or abuse. (See Webster's dictionary, or ask any married man.) ~ ASSAIL
A12. What ice cream manufacturers do when they locate on Wrangle Island. ~ FAIL
A14. This may talk, but it never gives itself away (slang). ~ KALE
A16. Preposition. ~ AT
A18. What women are when they talk too much. (Including the Scandinavian.) ~ TALKATIVE
A21. Pronoun. ~ WE
A22. You can get a kick out of this. ~ LEG
A24. This is the cause and effect of crosswords. ~ IRE
A25. This is something that schooners used to sail across. (Careful now!) ~ SEA
A26. A crossword puzzle "burden." This has nothing to do with the fact that a burden the hand is worth two in the bush. ~ ONUS
A27. A pronoun. ~ US
A28. In like manner. ~ AS
A30. What prize fighters do for an opening. ~ SPAR
A31. The kind of poem that poets write to landlords. ~ ODE
A32. Declares. ~ ASSERTS
A35. What small boys would rather be than go to school. ~ ILL
A36. It isn't necessary to study aviation to fly in this. ~ RAGE
A38. Some horses are dapple grays, but this is a horse of another color. ~ ROAN
A40. A vase. ~ URN
A41. The kind of shoes policemen wear. ~ LARGE
A42. This, according to Mr. Webster, is a system or theory. ~ ISM
A44. An objectionable kind of rumor or roomer. ~ IDLE
A46. This is crusty and sometimes full of applesauce. ~ TART
A49. This is what Francis the First said was lost. ~ ALL
A51. A very large eggplant. ~ OSTRICH
A54. Humpty-Dumpty. ~ EGG
A56. Flivvers are called crapshooters' specials because they shake, rattle, and do this. ~ ROLL
A57. Adverb, in like manner. ~ SO
A58. Pipe down! ~ SH
A59. Self-made men are men who do this and self-made widows are widows who got tired of listening to them. ~ BRAG
A60. This meant "eggs" in the days of Caesar and as Latin students will remember he once told Brutus he "et tu." ~ OVA
A62. To have and to hold. ~ OWN
A65. The wages of this are paid with a hangover; and a high rate of interest. ~ SIN
A66. I and the boy friend. (Correct this sentence!) ~ ME
A67. The fate of pedestrians. ~ DEPLETION
A71. This means No in the King's English. ~ NO
A72. This is a dangerous kind of a driver to be hit by. ~ PILE
A73. A legal claim to property. ~ LIEN
A75. The resting place of the old-fashioned cookie jar. ~ PANTRY
A76. What newspapers do when they can't say for certain. (Dumb Dora thinks this is what doughnuts are.) ~ ALLEGE

D2. A preposition. ~ OF
D3. What ball players are often up to. ~ BAT
D4. What feminine tears do to a male jury. ~ BIAS
D5. Where bad Englishmen go. ~ ELL
D7. A Norwegian flivver. ~ SKI
D8. Some men do this for a rainy day and then spend it all on a wet night. ~ SAVE
D9. Why men go to Canada and "See America Thirst." ~ ALE
D10. That is (abbr.). ~ IE
D11. The place where men forgot. ~ SALOON
D13. The definition of 25 Horizontal covers this word too. ~ BAR
D15. The right way to subscribe to JUDGE. ~ YEARLY
D17. What good bartenders do. ~ TEND
D19. This is putting two and two together. ~ KISS
D20. Girls never shed this when they get a new fur coat. ~ TEAR
D21. Welfare. ~ WEAL
D23. Kind of warfare indulged in by Mexicans and rum runners. ~ GUERRILLA
D25. These are people who either didn't embrace their. their opportunities or wouldn't let their opportunities embrace them. ~ SPINSTERS
D27. If memory serves, this is what Solomon said arguing with the dumb woman was. (Try and find the superfluous word in that sentence.) ~ USELESS
D29. Something that rubber necks do. ~ STRETCH
D32. Agriculture (abbr.). ~ AG
D33. What some people are in most of the time and most people are in some of the time. ~ ERROR
D34. A note in the diatonic scale. ~ SO
D37. This separates ham and eggs. ~ AND
D39. This is what hot air really is. ~ AIR
D43. A billiard player's kisses. ~ CAROMS
D45. The poor Indian again. ~ LO
D47. An interjection of satisfaction. ~ AH
D48. If it weren't for ambitious chickens this drink simply wouldn't exist. ~ EGGNOG
D50. Life is just one darn thing after another. This is just one darn fool after another. ~ LOVE
D52. What a can opener is to a kitchen mechanic. ~ TOOL
D53. A contracted equivalent of "ain't." ~ ISNT
D55. Object of all dealers in gold bricks. ~ GAIN
D61. A pessimist wears both this and suspenders. ~ BELT
D63. The trouble with the dry laws seems to be that they're all this way. ~ WET
D64. A tramp's idea of capital punishment. ~ TOIL
D67. Found in boiler factories. ~ DIN
D68. Prefix meaning through. ~ PER
D69. The wind that blows no one good. ~ ILL
D70. Born. ~ NEE
D72. The original floorwalker. ~ PA
D74. No good (abbr.). ~ NG


